from core import L
L(1,'boolean','Boolean algebra and algebraic minimization','Boolean algebra;Algebraic technique;SOP;POS','Propositional logic and binary values.',
'Boolean simplification preserves a function while reducing the gates required to implement it. Work with identities valid for every input. Boolean addition is OR, so 1+1=1; ordinary numerical cancellation cannot be imported without checking it.',
'''Basic identities::Identity laws give A+0=A and A·1=A; domination gives A+1=1 and A·0=0. Complement gives A+¬A=1 and A¬A=0. Idempotence gives A+A=A and AA=A. Absorption A+AB=A removes a redundant condition. De Morgan moves negation through a gate and swaps AND with OR.
||Canonical forms::A minterm specifies every variable and is true on exactly one truth-table row. Sum the minterms where the function is 1 to obtain canonical SOP. A maxterm is false on exactly one row; multiply maxterms for rows where the function is 0 to obtain canonical POS. Ordinary simplified SOP need not contain every variable in every term.
||Simplifying systematically::Combine terms differing in only one literal: AB+A¬B=A(B+¬B)=A. Remove absorbed terms and apply the consensus rule XY+¬XZ+YZ=XY+¬XZ. Confirm the result with a truth table when unsure. Fewer literals and fewer gates are related but distinct implementation cost measures.''',
'''Simplify F=AB+A¬B+AC::Factor the first two terms: A(B+¬B)=A.~Now F=A+AC.~Absorption removes AC, leaving F=A for all inputs.
||Build SOP for a 2-variable function true on 01 and 11::The corresponding minterms are ¬AB and AB.~F=¬AB+AB=B(¬A+A).~The simplified expression is B, so the output ignores A.''',
'Boolean OR is not arithmetic addition.||A canonical term must contain every variable.',
'A+AB=A.||AB+A¬B=A.||¬(AB)=¬A+¬B.',
[('How many rows does a 3-input Boolean truth table have?',8,'Each input has two values.','2³=8.'),('How many input assignments make F=A true in a 3-variable function A,B,C?',4,'B and C remain unconstrained.','Fix A=1; B and C each have 2 choices, yielding 4.')])
L(1,'kmap-tabular','Karnaugh maps and the tabular method','Karnaugh map;Tabular method;Prime implicants;Dont cares','Boolean algebra; minterms.',
'Adjacent minterms differ in one variable, so combining them eliminates that variable. Karnaugh maps make this adjacency visual; the tabular method performs the same operation symbolically and scales without relying on a drawing.',
'''K-map layout and grouping::Use Gray-code labels such as 00,01,11,10 so neighbours differ in one bit. Group 1s in rectangles with 1,2,4,8,… cells. Edges wrap around and corners may be adjacent; diagonal cells are not adjacent. A group with 2ᵏ cells eliminates k variables. Overlap is allowed and often necessary; every required 1 must be covered.
||Prime and essential implicants::A prime implicant cannot be enlarged into another valid group. An essential prime implicant covers a minterm covered by no other prime implicant and must be selected. After selecting essentials, cover remaining minterms with a low-cost collection. Don’t-care cells may be included as 1s to enlarge a group or ignored; there is no requirement to cover them.
||Quine–McCluskey::Write minterms in binary, group by number of 1s, and combine terms differing in one specified bit, replacing that bit by a dash. Repeat until no more combinations are possible. Uncombined terms are prime implicants. Build a chart mapping primes to covered required minterms and select a cover. This separates implicant generation from the covering problem.''',
'''Minimize F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,2,3)::The minterms are 000,001,010,011.~Across the four-cell group B and C take both values but A stays 0.~Eliminate B,C to get F=¬A.
||Combine minterms 1010 and 1011 tabularly::They differ only in the final bit.~Replace that position by a dash: 101−.~For variables A,B,C,D the product is A¬BC; D is eliminated.''',
'K-map labels use Gray order, not binary numeric order.||Larger groups should not include required zero cells.',
'A 2ᵏ-cell group eliminates k literals.||Essential implicants cover uniquely covered required minterms.',
[('A group of 8 cells in a 4-variable K-map leaves how many literals?',1,'Eight is 2³.','Eliminate 3 of 4 variables; 1 literal remains.'),('How many differing specified bits may be combined in one Quine–McCluskey step?',1,'Combining removes one bit position.','Exactly one differing specified bit is replaced by a dash.')])
L(1,'combinational','Designing combinational circuits','Combinational circuits;Adders;Multiplexers;Decoders','Boolean expressions and truth tables.',
'A combinational circuit has no memory: outputs are determined by current inputs. The design process starts with a behavioural specification, translates it to a truth table, simplifies the output equations, and maps them to available gates or modules.',
'''Adders and arithmetic building blocks::A half adder produces sum A⊕B and carry AB. A full adder includes Cin: sum=A⊕B⊕Cin and Cout=AB+ACin+BCin. Cascading full adders yields a ripple-carry adder whose worst delay grows with the carry path length. A carry-lookahead adder uses generate G=AB and propagate P=A⊕B to compute carry dependencies more directly.
||Multiplexers and decoders::A 2ᵏ:1 multiplexer selects one of 2ᵏ data inputs using k select bits. A decoder maps k inputs to up to 2ᵏ one-hot outputs. Implement a Boolean function with a mux by assigning select variables, then making each data input the residual function of remaining variables. Decoder minterm outputs can be ORed for SOP implementation.
||Design and timing::Validate all input combinations, including active-high/active-low control conventions. Gates have propagation delays; the longest input-to-output path determines worst-case settling. Logical equivalence alone does not remove hazards caused by unequal physical path delays. Combinational feedback without carefully defined storage is not ordinary combinational design.''',
'''Full adder with A=1,B=1,Cin=0::Sum=1⊕1⊕0=0.~Carry=AB+ACin+BCin=1+0+0=1.~The pair Cout,S represents binary 10, correctly summing decimal 2.
||Implement XOR using a 2:1 mux::Select S=A.~If A=0, output should equal B, so I₀=B.~If A=1, output should equal ¬B, so I₁=¬B. The mux equation ¬AI₀+AI₁ yields XOR.''',
'Output bit width must allow carry if the specification requires it.||Check active-low enables before using a decoder table.',
'Full-adder sum=A⊕B⊕Cin.||A 2ᵏ-input mux needs k selectors.',
[('How many select lines does a 16:1 multiplexer require?',4,'Solve 2ᵏ=16.','k=4.'),('How many output lines does a complete 3-to-8 decoder have?',8,'One output per input combination.','2³=8 outputs.')])
L(1,'sequential','Sequential circuits, flip-flops and state machines','Sequential circuits;Flip flops;Counters;Moore;Mealy','Combinational circuits and binary arithmetic.',
'Sequential circuits combine current inputs with stored state. To reason correctly, distinguish what is stored now, what will be stored at the next clock event, and which outputs depend on inputs immediately.',
'''Storage elements::A level-sensitive latch can change while its enable is active; an edge-triggered flip-flop samples around a clock edge. For D flip-flops Qnext=D; for T flip-flops Qnext=Q⊕T. JK devices hold for 00, reset for 01, set for 10 and toggle for 11. Setup and hold intervals constrain how stable inputs must be around the sampling edge.
||State-machine construction::List states and draw input-labelled transitions. A Moore output depends only on state; a Mealy output depends on state and current input. Assign binary state codes, derive next-state/output truth tables, simplify logic and select storage. k flip-flops can encode at most 2ᵏ states, so n states require ceiling(log₂n) bits.
||Counters and timing::A synchronous counter clocks all flip-flops together; ripple counters cascade transitions and have accumulated delays. A modulo-n counter uses n reachable states and returns to its start. Include reset behaviour and handle unused state codes so a stray state does not trap the machine. Clock period must allow launch, combinational delay and destination setup margin.''',
'''Design the state capacity of a modulo-10 counter::Need 10 distinct states.~Three bits encode only 8; four encode 16.~Use 4 flip-flops and define transitions for the 6 unused codes, typically returning to a legal state.
||Track a T flip-flop starting at Q=0 for inputs 1,0,1::First edge toggles Q to 1.~Second edge with T=0 retains 1.~Third edge toggles to 0; the final stored state is 0.''',
'A latch and a flip-flop are not interchangeable timing models.||Unused binary encodings still need recovery behaviour.',
'D: Qnext=D; T: Qnext=Q⊕T.||State bits=ceiling(log₂n).',
[('How many flip-flops are needed to encode 9 states?',4,'Find the smallest power of two at least 9.','2³=8 is insufficient; 2⁴=16, so 4 bits.'),('How many states can 5 flip-flops encode?',32,'Every bit has two possibilities.','2⁵=32 states.')])
L(1,'representation','Fixed point, floating point and overflow','Number representation;Fixed point;Floating point;Arithmetic','Binary notation and powers of two.',
'Bits do not have an inherent numeric interpretation. The same pattern may represent unsigned data, a signed integer, or a fraction depending on the agreed encoding. Arithmetic questions must specify width, sign convention and scaling before calculation.',
'''Integer representations::Unsigned n-bit values span 0 to 2ⁿ−1. Two’s complement spans −2ⁿ⁻¹ to 2ⁿ⁻¹−1; its most significant bit has weight −2ⁿ⁻¹. Negate within a fixed width by complementing all bits and adding 1. Sign extension repeats the sign bit to preserve a two’s-complement value. Carry-out concerns unsigned overflow; signed addition overflows when equal-sign operands produce an opposite-sign result.
||Fixed-point scaling::With f fractional bits, interpret the stored integer as I/2ᶠ. An unsigned n-bit fixed-point format has step size 2⁻ᶠ and maximum (2ⁿ−1)/2ᶠ. Signed formats use a signed stored integer with the same scaling. Addition requires aligned scaling; multiplying two scaled integers doubles the fractional-bit count until rescaled.
||IEEE 754 single precision::There are 1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits and 23 stored fraction bits. Normal numbers have value (−1)ˢ(1.f)2^(E−127). E=0 reserves zero and subnormal values; E=255 reserves infinities and NaNs. The implicit leading 1 applies only to normals. Rounding means real-number associativity need not hold numerically.''',
'''Encode −5 in 8-bit two’s complement::Write +5 as 00000101.~Invert: 11111010; add 1: 11111011.~Decode with negative MSB weight: −128+64+32+16+8+2+1=−5.
||Interpret a fixed-point stored value 24 with 4 fractional bits::The scale is 2⁴=16.~Actual value=24/16=1.5.~A one-unit change in stored value changes the real value by 1/16, not by 1.''',
'Two’s-complement range is asymmetric.||The implicit leading 1 is absent for subnormal floats.||Carry and signed overflow are different.',
'Signed range: −2ⁿ⁻¹ … 2ⁿ⁻¹−1.||Fixed point: value=integer/2ᶠ.||Float bias for single precision=127.',
[('What is the largest signed 8-bit two’s-complement integer?',127,'Use 2⁷−1.','The range is −128 to 127.'),('Stored integer 40 uses 3 fractional bits. What value does it represent?',5,'Divide by 2³.','40/8=5.')])
L(2,'instructions','Instruction sets, addressing modes and CPU time','Instruction set;Addressing modes;CPU performance','Binary arithmetic and memory addresses.',
'An instruction is a compact command that tells a processor what operation to perform and where operands come from. Addressing modes separate operand-location rules from the operation itself. CPU performance follows the total work and cycles, not clock rate alone.',
'''Instruction structure::An instruction can contain an opcode, register identifiers, addressing-mode bits and immediate/displacement fields. Load/store architectures generally operate on registers and use separate memory-transfer instructions; other architectures may permit memory operands directly. The program counter identifies instruction flow, and branches update it differently from ordinary sequential execution.
||Effective addresses::Immediate addressing embeds the operand, not an address. Register addressing reads the register value. Direct addressing uses an explicit address; indirect addressing follows a pointer to the operand. Base+displacement computes EA=base+offset; indexed modes add an index, sometimes scaled by element size. PC-relative addressing uses a displacement from a specified PC reference point; conventions matter.
||Performance equation::CPU time=instruction count×average CPI×cycle time=IC×CPI/frequency. For instruction classes, average CPI=Σfractionᵢ×CPIᵢ. A new design may use fewer instructions but more cycles each. Compare complete execution time and use speedup=old time/new time.''',
'''Address an array element using base 1000, index 3 and 4-byte elements::The byte displacement is 3×4=12.~EA=1000+12=1012.~Using raw index 3 as a byte offset would fetch the wrong location.
||Compute time for 2 million instructions, CPI 3, clock 1 GHz::Total cycles=2×10⁶×3=6×10⁶.~Cycle time is 1/10⁹ seconds.~CPU time=0.006 s=6 ms.''',
'Bytes, words and elements are different address units.||Average CPI must weight instruction frequencies.',
'EA=base+scaled index+displacement.||Time=IC×CPI/frequency.',
[('With base 2000 and byte displacement 24, what is the effective address?',2024,'Add the displacement.','2000+24=2024.'),('A program needs 6 million cycles at 2 GHz. What is its time in milliseconds?',3,'Divide cycles by cycles per second, then convert.','6×10⁶/(2×10⁹)=0.003 seconds=3 ms.')])
L(2,'alu-control','ALU design and hardwired/microprogrammed control','Design of ALU;Hardwired control;Microprogrammed control','Combinational logic; instructions; registers.',
'The datapath performs operations; the control unit chooses which operation happens and when. Keeping those roles separate makes it possible to trace an instruction as a sequence of register transfers instead of treating the CPU as a black box.',
'''ALU and status flags::An ALU combines an adder, logic functions and selection hardware. Subtraction can reuse addition by complementing the second operand and setting carry-in: A−B=A+¬B+1. Flags may record zero, sign, carry and signed overflow. Signed comparison cannot rely on the sign of a wrapped subtraction without considering overflow.
||Register-transfer sequencing::An instruction fetch can involve MAR←PC, memory read, IR←memory data and PC advancement. Execution then reads operands, activates the ALU, and writes a destination. Control signals choose mux inputs, register write enables, ALU function and memory actions. A single-cycle design combines actions in one long cycle; multicycle designs reuse hardware across shorter steps.
||Control implementation::Hardwired control uses finite-state logic and decoding to generate signals, often with low latency but less flexibility. Microprogrammed control stores control words in a control memory; a sequencer chooses subsequent microinstructions using opcode and conditions. Horizontal microcode exposes more parallel control bits, while encoded vertical microcode reduces width at the cost of decoding.''',
'''Compute 5−3 using 4-bit adder hardware::Represent 5=0101 and 3=0011.~Invert 3 to 1100 and add carry-in 1: 0101+1100+1=10010.~Discard the carry beyond 4 bits; result 0010 is 2. The carry flag and signed-overflow flag are interpreted separately.
||Estimate control-store capacity::Suppose there are 128 microinstructions, each 40 bits wide.~Storage is 128×40=5120 bits.~Divide by 8 to obtain 640 bytes; sequencing circuitry is additional hardware.''',
'Microinstructions and machine instructions are different levels.||Overflow interpretation depends on signedness.',
'A−B=A+¬B+1 at fixed width.||Datapath computes; control selects and sequences.',
[('A control store has 256 words of 32 bits. How many bytes does it occupy?',1024,'Multiply words by bits per word, then divide by 8.','256×32/8=1024 bytes.'),('How many bits are needed to select one of 8 ALU operations?',3,'Use log₂ of the number of choices.','2³=8, so 3 selection bits.')])
L(2,'cache','Memory hierarchy and cache mapping','Memory hierarchy;Cache mapping;Cache performance','Binary address splitting and CPU performance.',
'Caches exploit repetition and neighbourhood in memory accesses. A small fast store holds copies of slower memory blocks. Mapping decides where a block may go; replacement decides which resident block leaves when those allowed locations are full.',
'''Hierarchy and locality::Temporal locality reuses recently accessed data; spatial locality accesses nearby addresses. Transfers occur in blocks, so one miss may bring several adjacent words. A larger block can improve spatial reuse but reduces the number of resident blocks at fixed capacity and increases transfer cost. Separate compulsory, capacity and conflict misses when reasoning about changes.
||Address partition::In a byte-addressed system with 2ᵇ-byte blocks, the lowest b bits are block offset. A cache with S sets needs log₂S index bits; remaining high bits form the tag. Direct mapping has one line per set; W-way associativity has W lines per set. Sets=capacity/(block size×ways). Fully associative caches have one set and no index bits.
||Writes and performance::Write-through updates lower memory on writes; write-back marks lines dirty and writes them on eviction. Write-allocate versus no-write-allocate controls write misses independently. Single-level AMAT=hit time+miss rate×miss penalty, where the penalty is the additional miss cost. Multilevel formulas require using local versus global miss rates consistently.''',
'''Split a 32-bit address for a 16 KiB, 4-way cache with 64-byte blocks::Total lines=16384/64=256; sets=256/4=64.~Offset bits=log₂64=6; index bits=log₂64=6.~Tag bits=32−6−6=20. Capacity alone does not determine index bits without associativity.
||Compute AMAT for hit time 2 ns, miss rate 4%, penalty 50 ns::Miss contribution is 0.04×50=2 ns.~Add the baseline hit time 2 ns.~AMAT=4 ns; do not multiply the hit time by hit rate in this penalty-based convention.''',
'Cache capacity usually excludes tag/valid/dirty storage unless specified.||Use number of sets, not total lines, for index bits.',
'Index bits=log₂sets; offset bits=log₂block bytes.||AMAT=hit+miss rate×extra penalty.',
[('How many offset bits does a 128-byte block require?',7,'Find log₂128.','128=2⁷, so 7 bits.'),('A hit takes 1 ns, miss rate 0.1, extra penalty 30 ns. What is AMAT in ns?',4,'Use the penalty convention.','1+0.1×30=4 ns.')])
L(2,'memory-io','Memory interfacing, interrupts and DMA','Memory interfacing;I/O interface;Interrupt;DMA','Addresses, buses and control signals.',
'A processor must select the correct memory device and coordinate data transfer. I/O introduces devices whose speed and readiness differ from the CPU. Polling, interrupts and DMA assign different amounts of coordination work to software and hardware.',
'''Memory organization and decoding::A memory specified as N×w stores N locations of w bits each. Address bits select locations; data pins carry width w. To expand width, connect chips to different data-bit lanes while sharing address lines. To expand depth, decode extra high address bits into chip-select signals. Byte addressing may require multiple byte lanes and enables.
||Polling and interrupts::Polling repeatedly checks a device status register and spends CPU cycles waiting. An interrupt lets a device request attention; the CPU saves required execution state, enters an interrupt handler, services the event and returns. Masking, priority and nesting determine which interrupts can be serviced. Interrupt latency includes finishing or suspending permitted work and reaching the handler.
||DMA::A DMA controller transfers blocks between a device and memory after the CPU configures addresses, length and direction. Burst mode may occupy a bus for a sequence; cycle stealing interleaves transfers with CPU activity. DMA reduces per-byte CPU overhead but does not imply zero setup cost or unlimited bandwidth. Shared caches can require coherency handling for DMA buffers.''',
'''Build 4K×16 memory from 1K×8 chips::Width expansion requires 16/8=2 chips in parallel per bank.~Depth expansion requires 4K/1K=4 banks.~Total=2×4=8 chips; two extra address bits select the bank, while ten bits select a location inside a chip.
||Compare one-byte interrupts with DMA for 4096 bytes::An interrupt per byte produces 4096 service events.~A DMA block can use one setup and a completion interrupt under the simple model.~The CPU saves many handler invocations, although memory-bus transfer time remains.''',
'Chip width and depth expansion are separate factors.||DMA still competes for memory bandwidth.',
'Total chips=(desired depth/chip depth)×(desired width/chip width).||Interrupts request service; DMA performs data movement.',
[('How many 2K×8 chips build an 8K×16 memory?',8,'Multiply depth and width factors.','(8K/2K)×(16/8)=4×2=8.'),('How many address bits select one location in a 4K-location chip?',12,'4K=4096.','4096=2¹², requiring 12 address bits.')])
L(2,'pipeline','Instruction pipelining and hazards','Instruction pipelining;Pipeline hazards;Forwarding;Branches','Instruction sequencing and CPU cycles.',
'Pipelining overlaps different instructions in different stages. It increases completed instructions per unit time, while each individual instruction still passes through every stage. Hazards arise when that ideal overlap would violate resource limits or program dependencies.',
'''Ideal timing::For k equal-latency stages and n instructions, an initially empty ideal pipeline takes k+n−1 cycles. The first instruction fills the pipeline; each subsequent one then finishes one cycle later. Clock period is determined by the slowest stage plus pipeline-register overhead, not average stage delay. Unequal stages reduce ideal speedup.
||Data and structural hazards::A structural hazard occurs when simultaneous operations need the same unavailable resource. A RAW dependency requires a consumer to receive the producer’s value; forwarding can route it before register writeback. A load-use pair can still stall if data arrives after the consumer needs it. WAR and WAW are naming/order hazards relevant to designs that reorder reads or writes; simple in-order pipelines handle them differently.
||Control hazards::A branch may make fetched instructions wrong-path work. Waiting, predicting, or delayed-branch mechanisms handle this in different models. A misprediction flushes invalid instructions and adds cycles. To compute effective CPI, add expected stall cycles per instruction from hazards without double-counting overlapping penalties.''',
'''A 5-stage pipeline runs 12 instructions with 3 total stall cycles::Ideal cycles=5+12−1=16.~Add the specified actual stalls: 16+3=19.~At 2 ns per cycle total elapsed time is 38 ns, not 12×5×2.
||A branch frequency is 20%, misprediction rate 10%, penalty 3 cycles::Mispredictions per instruction=0.2×0.1=0.02.~Expected additional CPI=0.02×3=0.06.~If baseline CPI is 1 and there are no other stalls, effective CPI=1.06.''',
'Dependency does not automatically mean a stall if forwarding meets timing.||Pipeline speedup is not simply stage count for short sequences.',
'Cycles=k+n−1+actual penalties.||Clock period follows the slowest stage.',
[('A 4-stage ideal pipeline runs 10 instructions. How many cycles are needed?',13,'Fill then finish one per cycle.','4+10−1=13.'),('A sequence ideally needs 20 cycles and adds 4 nonoverlapping stalls. What is its total?',24,'Add actual stalls once.','20+4=24 cycles.')])
